      id: 'dp-hcm-14',
      title: 'Passage 14',
      difficulty: 'Medium',
      text: "As of August 11, 2025, a major development dominating India's economic news is the introduction of the Revised New Income Tax Bill 2025 in Parliament. Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman tabled the bill in the Lok Sabha, marking a significant overhaul of the country's taxation framework. This comes after the government withdrew the initial draft presented in February 2025, incorporating extensive feedback from a Parliamentary Select Committee. The bill aims to replace the outdated Income-Tax Act of 1961 with a modern, simplified code that emphasizes fairness, clarity, and efficiency in tax administration. Experts hail it as a step toward reducing litigation, broadening the tax base, and adapting to the digital economy, potentially affecting millions of taxpayers, businesses, and investors. The original bill, introduced earlier this year, faced criticism for ambiguities and omissions, prompting the formation of a 31-member Select Committee chaired by BJP MP Baijayant Panda. The committee submitted a comprehensive report with 285 recommendations, many of which have been integrated into the revised version. Sitharaman emphasized that the withdrawal of the February draft was to avoid confusion from parallel versions and ensure a unified, stakeholder-informed legislation. The revised bill adjusts tax slabs and rates across categories to benefit all taxpayers, with a focus on easing compliance for individuals and small businesses while strengthening provisions for digital transactions and dispute resolution. Key highlights from the bill include a revised tax structure designed to promote equity. While specific slab details are still being debated in Parliament, preliminary indications suggest adjustments to income thresholds: For instance, the basic exemption limit may rise from 3 lakh to 3.5 lakh under the new regime, with progressive rates starting at 5% for incomes up to 7 lakh, 10% up to 10 lakh, and higher brackets seeing marginal relief to counter inflation. This aims to provide relief to middle-class earners amid rising living costs. Corporate tax rates remain competitive at 22% for domestic companies opting out of exemptions, but new incentives for startups and MSMEs include extended loss carry-forward periods. A cornerstone of the bill is the integration of digital taxation measures. In response to the booming e-commerce and gig economy, the legislation introduces provisions for taxing digital services and cryptocurrencies more robustly. For example, a 2% equalization levy on non-resident digital firms could expand, and virtual digital assets (VDAs) will face clearer guidelines, including a 1% TDS on transfers above 50,000. This aligns with global standards like OECD's Pillar Two, ensuring India captures revenue from multinational tech giants without stifling innovation. Dispute resolution sees substantial reforms to address the backlog of over 5 lakh pending cases. The bill proposes faceless appeals and assessments powered by AI-driven analytics, reducing human intervention and biases. A new Dispute Resolution Committee (DRC) for small taxpayers (disputes under Rs20 lakh) will offer quicker settlements, with penalties waived for bona fide errors. Additionally, the Vivad Se Vishwas scheme may be revived with enhanced incentives, allowing taxpayers to settle old disputes at reduced rates. The bill's implications are far-reaching. For individuals, it promises lower effective taxes and easier filing, potentially boosting disposable incomes and consumption. Businesses anticipate reduced compliance costs-estimated to drop by 20%-and fewer audits, encouraging"
