      id: 'dp-hcm-30',
      title: 'Passage 30',
      difficulty: 'Medium',
      text: "It's a cliche in cricket: numbers aren't everything. Yet, they could become everything if the Board of Control for Cricket in India (BCCI) ratifies a proposed performance-based pay structure, floated during a review meeting following India's defeat in Australia in early 2025. Under this proposal, the number of zeros on players' cheques would directly correspond to their wickets taken or runs scored. Failure to meet predefined performance standards could lead to pay cuts, with the implied logic that financial uncertainty will keep players on their toes, sustain their motivation, and prevent mid-career stagnation. However, this variable-pay idea is a non-cricketing solution to a cricketing problem, one that risks undermining the collective spirit and endeavor that define team sports. Indian cricket, grappling with recent setbacks, needs ideas to restore its red-ball glory, not algebraic equations that reduce performance to cold metrics. Unlike the corporate world, where targets are often clear-cut, cricket's fascinating complexity defies simplistic numerical evaluation. A century scored in the second innings on a flat pitch against tiring bowlers may pale in comparison to a gritty fourth-innings forty on a turning track. A pacer's three-wicket burst on a lifeless pitch could outweigh a six-wicket haul on a green-top. How would an umpiring error leading to a low score be factored into the accounts? Cricket is shaped by too many variables - pitch conditions, weather, opposition strength, and sheer luck to be confined to a spreadsheet. If the BCCI adopts this recommendation, it risks transforming red-ball cricket into a reality show, where individual obsession overshadows team ideals. The board might need to hire a retinue of performance analysts, armed with the sacred 5Ws and 1H framework - who, what, when, where, why, and how - to dissect and grade every innings, spell, or catch. This could erode the soul of Test cricket, where selflessness and adaptability often matter more than raw statistics. A batter grinding out a low-scoring draw to save a match or a bowler tying down an end to frustrate the opposition might not generate headline numbers but can be pivotal to a team's success. Yet, under a pay-for-performance model, such contributions could be undervalued, pushing players to prioritize personal stats over team goals. The proposal also risks accelerating the exodus of talent from red-ball cricket to the lucrative franchise universe of T20 leagues, where financial rewards are already more predictable and substantial. Young cricketers, already lured by the glamour of the Indian Premier League (IPL), might see little incentive to toil in the five-day format if their earnings hinge on unpredictable metrics. The lessons from Indian cricket's past offer a better path forward. The team's golden era in Test cricket from the late 2010s to early 2020s, when India dominated series at home and abroad was built on collective resilience, not individual scorecards. Players like Virat Kohli, Cheteshwar Pujara, and Jasprit Bumrah thrived because of a culture that valued grit, strategy, and unity. The 2021 Gabba victory against Australia, where a depleted Indian side chased down 328 on the final day, wasn't driven by financial incentives but by a shared commitment to defy the odds. Introducing a pay structure that ties earnings to wickets or runs could fracture this ethos, fostering a mercenary mindset where players"
