      id: 'dp-hcm-33',
      title: 'Passage 33',
      difficulty: 'Medium',
      text: "Royal Challengers Bengaluru (RCB) played the Indian Premier League's (IPL) inaugural game on April 18, 2008, at the M. Chinnaswamy Stadium, but it was a night that belonged to their rivals, Kolkata Knight Riders, with Brendon McCullum's unbeaten 158 overshadowing the hosts in their own backyard. Over the years, RCB, with its fiercely loyal fan base, promised much but delivered little, managing only three runner-up finishes in 17 seasons. The franchise's long wait for glory finally ended on Tuesday night, June 3, 2025, when Rajat Patidar's men clinched the IPL's 18th edition with a thrilling six-run victory over Punjab Kings in the summit clash at Ahmedabad's Narendra Modi Stadium. This triumph was not just a team victory but a tribute to Virat Kohli's enduring legacy. A constant presence since RCB's first game in 2008, Kohli has been the team's spine, outlasting stars like Rahul Dravid, AB de Villiers, Chris Gayle, and Ross Taylor. From a young talent to a patriarch, Kohli's achievements with the Indian national team as both player and captain are legendary, but the absence of an IPL trophy had always rankled him. On that surreal June night, the relief and joy coursing through him were unmistakable as RCB finally laid the ghosts of their past to rest. RCB's 2025 campaign was a masterclass in balance and resilience. Rajat Patidar's assured leadership, Kohli's experience, Krunal Pandya's choking spin spells, and Josh Hazlewood's incisive pace formed the core of a squad that gelled at the right moments. Other players chipped in with crucial cameos, ensuring RCB stayed in the hunt throughout the tournament. Punjab Kings, their final opponents, deserve immense credit for their spirited performance. Led by Shreyas Iyer, Punjab showcased chutzpah, pushing RCB to the brink in a nail-biting finale. Iyer's captaincy, marked by tactical acumen and composure, signals his growing stature, positioning him as a future leader for India's ODI team when stalwarts like Rohit Sharma and Kohli eventually step away from the format. Despite falling short, Punjab's campaign was a testament to their potential, reaching the final with flair and determination. The 2025 IPL also highlighted contrasting fortunes for other teams. Mumbai Indians, after a lukewarm start, surged late to secure a playoff berth alongside Gujarat Titans. However, the season mirrored the decline of a pedigreed outfit, Chennai Super Kings (CSK), which finished last a shocking fall for a team with five IPL titles. An aging M.S. Dhoni, despite his enduring charisma, couldn't stem the rot, as CSK struggled with inconsistent performances and a lack of depth in their squad. The team's reliance on veterans, coupled with injuries and poor form among younger players, exposed vulnerabilities that opponents exploited ruthlessly. This marked a low point for CSK, whose fans, accustomed to success, were left grappling with the reality of their team's slide. RCB's triumph was a redemption song, not just for the franchise but for Indian cricket's broader narrative. The IPL, with its glitz and high stakes, often amplifies individual performances, but RCB's victory underscored the power of collective effort. Kohli, at 36, exemplified longevity, balancing aggressive batting with mentorship for younger players like Patidar. Pandya's ability to control the middle overs and Hazlewood's knack for breaking partnerships were pivotal, but it was the team's unity that shone through. The lessons from India's cricketing past notably the 2021 Gabba Test win, where a depleted side triumphed through grit and teamwork - resonate in RCB's 2025"
