      id: 'dp-hcm-34',
      title: 'Passage 34',
      difficulty: 'Medium',
      text: "\"By now it will probably bore you to hear that there is no such thing as a boring presidential election in Iran. But I submit that this is true,\" Laura Secor writes in her stirring 2016 book, Children of Paradise: The Struggle for the Soul of Iran. The 2017 Iranian presidential election, held on May 19, might seem to challenge her assertion at first glance, given the apparent ease with which Hassan Rouhani secured re-election. Rouhani won 57% of the vote, decisively defeating his nearest rival, Ebrahim Raisi, who garnered 38.5%, thus avoiding the need for a run-off. This outcome was so clearly Rouhani's to lose that it would have required a spectacular misstep by his campaign or an overt intervention akin to the controversial 2009 election that denied Mir Hossein Mousavi the presidency - for him to be ousted. Rouhani, a seasoned political operator, and the Supreme Leader, Ayatollah Ali Khamenei, now more cautious about sparking protests than Iran's \"deep state\" was eight years prior, ensured the election proceeded smoothly, lacking the edginess typically associated with Iranian polls. Past elections, such as the reformist Mohammad Khatami's victories in 1997 and 2001, or the populist Mahmoud Ahmadinejad's upset over Akbar Hashemi Rafsanjani in 2005, or his alleged vote-stealing in 2009, were marked by high drama and unpredictability. Yet, for all its predictability and lack of theatrics, the 2017 election may prove to be far more consequential than its predecessors, particularly in the context of Iran's opaque power dynamics and the looming question of who will succeed Khamenei as Supreme Leader. Rouhani's landslide victory was no surprise. His first term, beginning in 2013, was defined by the landmark 2015 nuclear deal (the Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action, or JCPOA), which lifted crippling sanctions in exchange for curbs on Iran's nuclear program. This achievement bolstered his appeal among moderates and reformists, who saw him as a pragmatic leader capable of opening Iran to the world while navigating its complex theocratic system. Raisi, a hardline cleric with deep ties to the judiciary and the Revolutionary Guards, represented the conservative establishment but struggled to match Rouhani's broad support base. The absence of widespread protests or allegations of fraud, unlike in 2009 when Mousavi's defeat sparked the Green Movement, reflected both Rouhani's popularity and Khamenei's calculated restraint. Since taking over from Ruhollah Khomeini in 1989, Khamenei has presided over a deepening web of security, political, and electoral arrangements, creating a system where power is diffuse and decision-making opaque. Even the most dedicated Iran watchers struggle to pinpoint exactly where authority lies whether with the Supreme Leader, the Revolutionary Guards, or the unelected bodies like the Guardian Council. This complexity makes the 2017 election a critical moment, as it sets the stage for the eventual transition of power in Iran's most powerful office. Speculation about Khamenei's successor has intensified, given his age and reported health concerns in 2017. The Supreme Leader's role, enshrined in Iran's constitution, grants near-absolute authority over state affairs, from foreign policy to judicial appointments. Since 1989, Khamenei has consolidated this power, balancing factions within the regime - reformists, moderates, and hardliners - while maintaining the"
