      id: 'dp-hcm-35',
      title: 'Passage 35',
      difficulty: 'Medium',
      text: "British Prime Minister Theresa May has found herself in a political quagmire, echoing the missteps of her predecessor, David Cameron. For the second consecutive year, the Conservative Party has snatched defeat from the jaws of victory. In 2016, Cameron, riding the momentum of a comfortable election win in 2015, called for a referendum on Britain's membership in the European Union (EU). The unexpected vote to leave - Brexit - forced his resignation, paving the way for May to assume the premiership in July 2016. Determined to secure a stronger mandate for the looming Brexit negotiations, May triggered Article 50 of the Treaty on European Union in March 2017, formally initiating the UK's withdrawal process. In April, she called a snap general election, hoping to bolster her parliamentary majority and strengthen her hand in talks with the EU. However, her campaign was lackluster, marked by a critical misstep: a proposal to curb old-age pension entitlements, which alienated the Conservative Party's core voter base the elderly. Meanwhile, the Labour Party, led by Jeremy Corbyn, capitalized on the youth vote with a platform championing multiculturalism, pro-immigration policies, enhanced welfare, and nationalization of key infrastructure industries. When the election results were announced on June 9, 2017, the Conservative Party was stunned to find it had lost its slim majority in the House of Commons, falling approximately eight seats short of the 326 needed to form a government outright, securing 317 seats with 42.3% of the vote. Labour, in contrast, gained 30 seats, increasing its tally to 262 with a 40% vote share, a remarkable surge from 30.4% in 2015. May, as leader of the single-largest party, staked her claim to form a minority government, cobbling together a confidence-and-supply agreement with Northern Ireland's Democratic Unionist Party (DUP), which won 10 seats. This arrangement, however, left her authority severely weakened. The election, intended to fortify her position, instead exposed deep divisions within her party and eroded her credibility. Her campaign's focus on \"strong and stable leadership\" unraveled amid gaffes, notably the pension policy reversal, and a cautious media strategy that avoided direct debates with Corbyn, who connected authentically with younger voters through promises of free college and increased welfare spending. The campaign was further disrupted by two major terrorist attacks in Manchester and London Bridge which shifted focus to national security. Labour criticized Conservative cuts to police numbers, while May emphasized global cooperation against terrorism, but the narrative of her government's security failures gained traction, further denting her campaign. The election outcome plunged Brexit negotiations, set to begin just 11 days later on June 19, into uncertainty. May had sought a clear mandate for her vision of a \"hard Brexit,\" exiting the EU's single market and customs union to prioritize immigration control and sovereignty. However, the loss of her majority complicated her ability to pass Brexit-related legislation, forcing reliance on the DUP and exposing her to internal party dissent between pro- and anti-Brexit factions. EU leaders, already adopting a tough stance, were now even less inclined to offer concessions. German Chancellor Angela Merkel, having regained political stability after facing backlash for her 2015 refugee policy, dismissed British hopes of \"having their cake and eating it too,\" emphasizing that Brexit talks must prioritize EU unity. Her decision to accept over a "
