      id: 'dp-hcm-37',
      title: 'Passage 37',
      difficulty: 'Medium',
      text: "Kangaroo mother care (KMC) - an intervention where stable low-birthweight newborns are placed in skin-to-skin contact with their mothers and exclusively breastfed - has been globally recommended for infants who don't require respiratory support, intravenous fluids, or other intensive care and can accept oral feeds. While previous studies have demonstrated that KMC improves survival rates for babies weighing less than 2 kilograms at birth compared to standard hospital care, global data reveal a stark reality: only about 5% of eligible infants receive this life-saving intervention. In India, where approximately 40% of the world's low-birthweight babies are born, the need for effective interventions is particularly urgent. Compounding the challenge, there has been limited evidence on the impact of KMC when initiated at home, a critical gap given that many babies in developing countries are born at home or discharged from hospitals too early to benefit from hospital-based KMC. To address this, a team of researchers conducted a pioneering study in Haryana, with results published in The Lancet in 2025, showing that home-initiated KMC improved survival by 30% for infants up to 28 days of age and by 25% up to six months, compared to standard care. These findings underscore the potential of KMC to significantly reduce neonatal and infant mortality if scaled up for all low-birthweight infants. The study, led by Dr. Sarmila Mazumder, enrolled over 8,000 stable low-birthweight babies those weighing less than 2.25 kilograms from two districts in Haryana between 2015 and 2018. Participants were randomly assigned to either an intervention group, which received home-initiated KMC, or a control group receiving standard care. KMC was initiated at an average age of 33 hours and continued through the first month of life, supported by home visits from trained health workers. The babies were followed up at one, three, and six months to assess survival and other health outcomes. The results were striking: KMC not only reduced mortality but also offered benefits beyond preventing hypothermia, a common risk for low-birthweight infants. Dr. Mazumder emphasized that KMC enhances exclusive breastfeeding and extends breastfeeding duration, which are critical for infant health. Exclusive breastfeeding strengthens immunity, reduces infections, and supports growth, while the skin-to-skin contact fosters bonding, regulates the baby's temperature and heart rate, and promotes physiological stability. India's significant share of global low-birthweight births approximately 7.5 million annually highlights the urgency of scaling up KMC. In developing countries, many infants are born at home, particularly in rural areas, or discharged from hospitals within hours of birth due to resource constraints or cultural practices. These newborns often miss out on KMC initiation in clinical settings, making home-based interventions essential. Dr. Mazumder noted, \"In developing countries, even today, babies are born at home or discharged too soon without KMC initiation. It is imperative that such care is initiated at home.\" The Haryana study provides robust evidence that home-based KMC is feasible and effective, even in resource-limited settings. Health workers visited families to train mothers in proper skin-to-skin positioning, ensuring the baby was placed upright on the mother's chest for at least eight hours daily, and provided guidance on exclusive breastfeeding. These visits also addressed practical challenges, such as family resistance or lack of awareness, ensuring sustained"
