      text: "Once upon a time, in a splendid kingdom nestled among rolling hills and shimmering rivers, there lived a majestic royal elephant, a magnificent creature who resided within the opulent premises of the king's grand palace. This elephant, draped in ornate silks and revered by all, was deeply cherished by the king, who ensured it was lavishly fed with the finest sweet rice, perfumed with saffron and cooked to perfection, and treated with utmost care and respect. Near the elephant's grand shed, shaded by ancient banyan trees, lived a frail and emaciated dog, his ribs starkly visible beneath his patchy coat. This scrawny dog, barely noticed by the palace staff, was endlessly captivated by the tantalizing aroma of the rich, sweet rice prepared daily for the royal elephant. One fateful day, unable to resist the intoxicating scent any longer, the dog mustered his courage and stealthily crept into the elephant's shed, darting toward the massive creature's meal. To his delight, he found the rice as delectable as its fragrance promised, and emboldened by his success, he began sneaking in daily to partake in the feast. For days, the colossal elephant, engrossed in savoring the delicious food, remained oblivious to the tiny intruder nibbling at his trough. Over time, nourished by the nourishing rice, the dog grew stronger, his frame filling out, his coat gleaming with newfound vitality. Eventually, the elephant caught sight of the dog, now robust and bold, but instead of driving him away, the gentle giant, amused by the dog's audacity, allowed him to share the meal. What began as quiet tolerance blossomed into companionship, as the elephant found joy in the dog's spirited presence. The two became inseparable, eating together, resting side by side under the stars, and frolicking in the palace gardens. Their favorite pastime was when the elephant, with a playful gleam in his eye, would gently scoop the dog up in his trunk, swinging him back and forth as the dog yipped with delight. Their bond deepened, and neither could imagine a day without the other's company they were two souls intertwined in an unlikely but profound friendship. However, one day, a stranger, a merchant from a distant land, visited the palace and spotted the dog bounding happily near the elephant's shed. Approaching the elephant-keeper, he declared, \"I wish to buy this dog. Name your price.\" The keeper, though he had no rightful claim to the dog, saw an opportunity for profit and, without hesitation, struck a deal, pocketing a hefty sum. The merchant whisked the dog away to his far-off village, leaving the elephant bereft. The once-vibrant elephant sank into deep melancholy, his heart heavy with the absence of his dear friend. He lost all interest in his surroundings, refusing to eat the fragrant rice, drink from the sparkling water trough, or even bathe in the cool palace pools. His sorrow was palpable, his spirit dimmed by loneliness. Alarmed by the elephant's deteriorating condition, the keeper reported the matter to the king, conveniently omitting any mention of the dog's sale. The king, concerned for his beloved elephant, summoned his wise minister, renowned for his uncanny ability to understand the hearts of animals. \"Go to the elephant's shed,\" the king commanded, \"and uncover the cause of my elephant's distress.\" The minister, with his keen insight, set out to unravel the mystery, determined to restore the royal elephant's joy."
