      id: 'dp-hcm-43',
      title: 'Passage 43',
      difficulty: 'Medium',
      text: "The University Grants Commission's directive mandating that college and university lecturers dedicate a minimum of 22 hours per week to direct teaching stems not from pedagogical wisdom but from the pressures of budgetary cutbacks. At first glance, it may seem peculiar that teachers would object to what appears to be a modest 22-hour teaching requirement. However, a deeper examination reveals the significant demands placed on academics beyond classroom hours. Preparing high-quality lectures requires substantial time, often involving in-depth research, crafting detailed presentations, and updating course materials to reflect current knowledge. Additionally, conscientious academics must allocate time for their own research, which is critical to advancing scholarship and maintaining intellectual rigor. In practice, most dedicated lecturers already work well over 40 hours a week, balancing teaching, preparation, research, student mentoring, and administrative duties. In university systems worldwide, lecturers typically spend only 12 to 15 hours per week on direct teaching activities, allowing ample time for these other essential responsibilities. The contrast with India's new mandate highlights the unrealistic expectations placed on its educators. Compounding the issue, the working conditions for Indian academics are far from ideal. The average college lecturer in India lacks basic infrastructure, such as personal office space for preparation or student consultations. Where computers are available, reliable internet connectivity is often absent, hindering access to digital resources and research databases. Libraries, critical for academic work, are frequently underfunded and poorly stocked, with outdated collections that fail to support rigorous scholarship. Against this backdrop, the UGC has imposed further constraints: a complete freeze on permanent recruitment, the abolition of all posts vacant for over a year, and a mandate to reduce staff strength by 10 percent. These measures, driven by fiscal austerity, are designed to ensure that the reduced workforce compensates by increasing teaching hours, thereby maintaining the quantum of teaching. However, this approach inevitably compromises the quality of education. Overburdened lecturers, stretched thin by excessive teaching loads, will have less time for preparation, research, and student engagement, leading to a decline in the overall quality of academic work. While it's true that some college teachers in India fail to conduct classes regularly, addressing this issue requires targeted accountability measures, not blanket policies that penalize all educators. Increasing the required teaching hours across the board does little to address absenteeism; a truant teacher is likely to remain disengaged regardless of the mandated hours. The UGC and institutions must instead implement robust mechanisms to monitor attendance and performance, ensuring that those who shirk their duties are held accountable without punishing diligent educators. The broader context of India's higher education system underscores the unsound state of affairs. Years of sustained financial neglect have left most Indian universities and colleges unable to conduct meaningful research. Laboratories lack modern equipment, libraries are under-resourced, and faculty are stretched thin, juggling heavy teaching loads with minimal support. As student enrollment has surged dramatically, public investment in higher education has, in relative terms, declined. Between 1985 and 1997, while public expenditure on higher education as a percentage of total educational."
