      id: 'dp-hcm-47',
      title: 'Passage 47',
      difficulty: 'Medium',
      text: "Once upon a time, in a quiet village in rural India, there lived a farmer named Tuan who owned a modest plot of land. A kind and good-natured soul, Tuan resided in a simple hut with his wife and children, eking out a living by selling the crops he diligently cultivated. His small land yielded enough to sustain his family, but what set Tuan apart was his boundless compassion. He was always ready to help others, whether it was assisting a sick villager, supporting a grieving family after a death, or aiding the village doctor at night to prepare medicines. Tuan's selflessness earned him the love and respect of nearly everyone in the community. His tireless efforts to tend to the needs of others, often at his own expense, made him a beloved figure, a pillar of the village. Yet, not everyone shared this admiration. Tuan's neighbor, Juan, harbored a deep-seated hatred for him. Living on the adjacent plot, Juan was a stark contrast to Tuan. Lazy by nature, he put in minimal effort to cultivate his land, resulting in meager harvests each year. While Tuan worked tirelessly, sowing and reaping with care, Juan's fields lay underutilized, producing far fewer crops. During the harvest season, Tuan's diligence paid off with a handsome profit from selling his produce, while Juan struggled to make ends meet. This disparity fueled Juan's jealousy, which simmered beneath the surface until it erupted in a malicious act. One night, just days before Tuan was set to reap his harvest, Juan crept onto Tuan's land and set fire to his crops. Tuan and his family were asleep, unaware of the danger, but the alertness of another neighbor, who spotted the flames, saved much of the crop from total destruction. The community rallied to douse the fire, preserving a portion of Tuan's hard-earned yield. When the flames were extinguished, Tuan traced the fire's origin to Juan's land. Though unaware of Juan's animosity, Tuan chose not to confront him immediately. A man of patience and restraint, he decided to let the matter rest, resolving to act only if Juan repeated such a treacherous deed. That year, Tuan sold what remained of his crops at a decent price, but the loss of a significant portion to the fire meant his profits were far lower than usual. The setback weighed heavily on his heart, yet true to his nature, he kept his pain private, unwilling to burden others with his troubles or speak ill of Juan. His silence reflected his character, a quiet strength that prioritized peace over retribution, even in the face of personal loss. This tale of Tuan and Juan mirrors broader lessons from India's past, such as the resilience shown during the COVID-19 pandemic, when communities united to support the vulnerable despite scarce resources. Tuan's compassion and restraint echo the selflessness of frontline workers who prioritized collective well-being over personal gain. Juan's envy, however, reflects a darker side of human nature, seen in historical instances of communal discord, such as land disputes in rural India, where jealousy over prosperity often leads to sabotage. According to a 2023 report by the National Crime Records Bureau, over 15% of rural conflicts in states like Bihar and Uttar Pradesh stem from land-related envy, underscoring the real-world parallels to this story. Tuan's response to adversity highlights the power of forbearance, a quality vital for maintaining community harmony. By choosing not to retaliate, he prevented escalation, much like village panchayats that resolve disputes through dialogue rather than confrontation. However, his silence also points to a systemic issue: the lack of formal mechanisms to address such acts in rural settings."
